# Engineering Rules

## Mandatory Development Standards

### 1. Test-Driven Development (TDD)

- Define acceptance criteria and tests before implementation where practical.
- Write regression tests for confirmed bugs.

### 2. Unit Testing

- Test business logic, validations, edge cases, and error handling.
- Prefer covering folio posting, voids, board plans, rental conflicts, capacity, tenant isolation.

### 3. Integration Testing

- Verify API, database, authentication, authorization, and complete user workflows.
- Test failure scenarios as well as successful flows.

### 4. Security First

- Verify authentication and authorization on the server.
- Enforce tenant isolation for all tenant-owned data.
- Check input validation, injection, XSS, CSRF where applicable, rate limits, and file uploads.

### 5. Adversarial Review

- After completing a feature, review how it could fail or be exploited.
- Report severity, reproduction steps, impact, and recommended fixes.
- Do not modify code during an audit unless explicitly authorized.

### 6. Performance Testing

- Identify expensive database queries, N+1 queries, resource leaks, and bottlenecks.
- Run controlled load tests only in authorized environments.

### 7. Code Quality

- Follow existing architecture and repository conventions.
- Avoid unnecessary refactoring and unrelated changes.
- Never remove or weaken tests merely to pass CI.

### 8. Completion Criteria

- Run relevant tests and report actual results when verification is allowed.
- Document changed files, security findings, and remaining risks.
- Never claim success without verification.
